<section id="s-problem" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Coordinate-Wise Skew</div>
<h2>Why Flat Vector Optimization Warps Matrix Geometry</h2>
<p>Modern optimizers like Adam and AdamW operate under a fundamental assumption: all model parameters can be flattened into a single, continuous vector and updated independently. While coordinate-wise division by the square root of gradient variance ($v_t$) successfully stabilizes individual weight coordinates, it completely ignores the **two-dimensional structural geometry** of linear projection matrices.</p>

<p>Linear layers in neural networks are operators that act on vector spaces. By flattening these matrices and applying coordinate-wise updates, standard optimizers warp the underlying geometric transformations. During training, the **singular values** of standard weight matrices skew dramatically. A few directions learn extremely quickly and dominate the representation, while others are squished and freeze. This anisotropy forces the training path to undergo severe oscillations and limits the learning speed of the network.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Interactive: 2D Spatial Warping of Coordinate Updates</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Toggle optimizer to see update transformation anisotropy</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="grid-canvas"></canvas>
    <div class="grid-controls">
      <button class="grid-btn active" id="btn-grid-adam">AdamW Update (Non-Orthogonal)</button>
      <button class="grid-btn" id="btn-grid-muon">Muon Update (Orthogonal)</button>
      <span class="toc-collapsed-label" style="display:none" id="grid-status-text">Status: AdamW</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>As shown above, coordinate-wise updates in AdamW shear and squash space (anisotropic stretching), skewing singular values. An orthogonal update (like Muon) applies a conformal, shape-preserving transformation (pure rotation/scaling), ensuring that all directions in the weight space learn at the exact same rate.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-orthogonal" data-code="orthogonal">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Newton-Schulz Solution</div>
<h2>Approximating Matrix Orthogonalization with Matrix Multiplication</h2>
<p>To prevent singular value skew, we should ideally project our momentum updates onto the space of orthogonal matrices. Mathematically, given a momentum matrix $M$, the nearest orthogonal matrix is given by $U V^T$, where $U \Sigma V^T = \text{"{"}SVD{"}"}(M)$. However, computing Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) directly is extremely slow on GPU hardware because it requires iterative, sequential algorithms (such as QR or Jacobi sweeps).</p>

<p>To avoid SVD, the **Muon** optimizer uses a polynomial approximation called **Newton-Schulz iteration**. Starting from a normalized matrix $X_0$, it repeatedly applies a quintic (5th-degree) polynomial that drives the singular values of $X_k$ towards 1:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Newton-Schulz Quintic Recurrence</div>
  <span class="math-var">X<sub>k+1</sub></span> = 3.4445·<span class="math-var">X<sub>k</sub></span> + <span class="math-var">X<sub>k</sub></span>·(-4.7750·<span class="math-var">A<sub>k</sub></span> + 2.0315·<span class="math-var">A<sub>k</sub><sup>2</sup></span>) 
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">where A<sub>k</sub> = X<sub>k</sub><sup>T</sup>X<sub>k</sub> (or X<sub>k</sub>X<sub>k</sub><sup>T</sup> depending on matrix shape)</span>
</div>

<p>In practice, Muon runs exactly 5 steps of this iteration. Because the iteration consists entirely of matrix-matrix multiplications, it runs incredibly fast on GPU Tensor Cores, yielding a massive hardware speedup over direct SVD.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Interactive: Singular Value and Orthogonality Convergence</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Drag slider to watch matrix orthogonalize step-by-step</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="ns-canvas"></canvas>
    <div class="ns-controls">
      <div class="ns-slider-container">
        <span class="ns-label">Newton-Schulz Steps: <span id="ns-step-val" style="color:var(--cyan);font-weight:700">0</span></span>
        <input type="range" min="0" max="5" value="0" id="ns-slider">
      </div>
      <button class="grid-btn" id="btn-ns-random" style="padding:.25rem .6rem;font-size:.65rem">New Random Matrix</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>One fascinating detail is that the Newton-Schulz polynomial coefficients do not converge to exactly 1.0. Instead, they converge to a stable **2-cycle** oscillating between $\sigma_{"{"}min{"}"} \approx 0.68$ and $\sigma_{"{"}max{"}"} \approx 1.13$. In deep learning optimization, this is a feature, not a bug: it is computationally cheap, and reducing the singular value ratio (condition number) from $100+$ down to only $1.66$ is more than enough to stabilize learning.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-scaling" data-code="scaling">
<div class="section-no">03 — Scaling Up: Decay and LR</div>
<h2>Solving Precision Overflow and Shape-Dependent Updates</h2>
<p>While Keller Jordan's original Muon optimizer set speed records on small models like NanoGPT, attempts to train models with billions of parameters on trillions of tokens initially failed. In their technical report, the Moonshot AI team identified and solved two critical bottlenecks:</p>

<h3>1. The Precision Overflow (Weight Decay)</h3>
<p>Unlike AdamW, the original Muon implementation omitted weight decay. In long-running pretraining, the weights and the layer activation Root-Mean-Square (RMS) grow continuously without bound. This eventually causes the model representation to exceed the dynamic range of `bfloat16` precision, leading to massive training instability. Adding decoupled weight decay stabilizes weight growth:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Decoupled Weight Decay Update</div>
  <span class="math-var">W<sub>t</sub></span> = <span class="math-var">W<sub>t-1</sub></span> - <span class="math-var">η<sub>t</sub></span>(<span class="math-var">O<sub>t</sub></span> + <span class="math-var">λW<sub>t-1</sub></span>)
</div>

<h3>2. Shape-Aware Learning Rates</h3>
<p>For a full-rank matrix parameter of shape $[A, B]$, its theoretical Muon update RMS is $1 / \sqrt{"{"}\max(A, B){"}"}$. This causes a severe update scale discrepancy across layers: MLP layers (which are very wide, e.g., $H \times 4H$) receive tiny updates, while narrow parameters receive massive updates. To fix this, Moonshot scales the update by $\sqrt{"{"}\max(A, B){"}"}$, and introduces a global $0.2$ scaling factor to align Muon's updates with standard AdamW (typically $0.2 \to 0.4$):</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Shape-Aware Muon Scaling</div>
  <span class="math-var">W<sub>t</sub></span> = <span class="math-var">W<sub>t-1</sub></span> - <span class="math-var">η<sub>t</sub></span>(0.2 · <span class="math-var">O<sub>t</sub></span> · &radic;<span class="math-var">max(A, B)</span> + <span class="math-var">λW<sub>t-1</sub></span>)
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Diagnostics: Weight Norm Evolution and Shape Discrepancy</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Click options to see validation improvements</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="norm-canvas"></canvas>
    <div class="norm-controls">
      <button class="grid-btn active" id="btn-norm-decay">Weight Decay Impact</button>
      <button class="grid-btn" id="btn-norm-lr">Shape LR Adjustment</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="s-distributed" data-code="distributed">
<div class="section-no">04 — Distributed Muon</div>
<h2>Memory Optimal ZeRO-1 Integration</h2>
<p>Modern LLMs are trained across thousands of GPUs using ZeRO-1 optimizer partitioning. However, ZeRO-1 is natively designed for element-wise updates (like AdamW) where parameters can be updated in isolation. Since Muon requires the full gradient matrix to perform Newton-Schulz orthogonalization, vanilla ZeRO-1 cannot be used out-of-the-box.</p>

<p>Distributed Muon resolves this by introducing a hybrid communication step: it gathers partitioned momentum buffers across data-parallel ranks, runs the 5 steps of Newton-Schulz on the full matrix, and then discards the off-rank update elements before applying the update locally.</p>

<div class="step-list">
  <div class="step-box active" data-step="0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Reduce-Scatter Gradients</div>
      <div class="step-desc">All-reduce and partition the parameters' gradients across the Data Parallel (DP) ranks, producing local slice <code>g</code>.</div>
      <div class="step-code">g = reduce_scatter(G, dp_group)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Momentum Accumulation</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Accumulate gradient momentum locally in a partitioned buffer <code>m</code> to conserve GPU memory.</div>
      <div class="step-code">m = mu * m + g</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Data-Parallel Gather</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Gather the partitioned momentum slices back into a full momentum matrix <code>M</code> on all ranks in the DP group.</div>
      <div class="step-code">M = gather(m, dp_group)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Newton-Schulz Iteration</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Run 5 quintic iterations of Newton-Schulz on the gathered matrix <code>M</code> in high-speed <code>bfloat16</code>.</div>
      <div class="step-code">O = newton_schulz5(M)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="4">
    <div class="step-num">5</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Partitioned Update and All-Gather</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Slice out the local rank's update partition <code>o</code>, update local parameters, and all-gather the updated weights.</div>
      <div class="step-code">p_new = p - lr * (scale * o + wd * p)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev" disabled>Previous Step</button>
  <button id="step-next">Next Step</button>
  <span id="step-prog" style="font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 5</span>
</div>
</section>

